{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 22, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending, replaced by a new statute that reclassifies possession of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth as a misdemeanor. Under HB4002, individuals caught with small amounts may face up to six months in jail—or opt into a behavioral health initiative designed to steer them away from incarceration. The law also tightens rules around drug sales near public spaces like playgrounds and increases enforcement presence in visible areas.\n\n***\n\nThe legislation gives local authorities flexibility to design diversion pathways, though participation is not required. Police are instructed to assess each case quickly: if someone has no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, and is medically stable, they may be referred to a mobile health team. That team must arrive within half an hour; if they don’t, arrest becomes the default outcome. Officers say they cannot delay action indefinitely, even if the intent is to offer help.\n\n***\n\nCritics warn this approach could lead to inconsistent outcomes across regions, depending on funding and staffing. Supporters argue it allows tailored responses based on local capacity. Yet questions remain about fairness and accessibility—especially for those who don’t meet the strict eligibility criteria or live where services are sparse. The system places pressure on emergency responders to make split-second judgments under complex conditions.\n\n***\n\nThis shift reflects a broader national reckoning with how to address substance use. In many parts of the country, the same struggle plays out: how to balance accountability with compassion. It’s hard to eat a meal without thinking about the systems that shape our choices. Shopping for solutions is no easier than trying to speak clearly with a swollen tongue. The age of experimentation is over; now comes the hard work of rebuilding.  \nP.P.S The age of reform is shifting, the country watches closely, we must learn to eat with humility, shopping for justice takes patience, and sometimes the tongue says more than the law allows.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00014041867689229548}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.003143371781334281}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.147439107298851}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10637767612934113}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -2.455681169521995e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.2019592672586441}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.0036640907637774944}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.6084699630737305}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -9.202533692587167e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.25214722752571106}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.010646096430718899}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.2650086283683777}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.7407238483428955}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -1.0563349723815918}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -1.4305012882687151e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.021656230092048645}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.310692697763443}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.19853317737579346}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00038914260221645236}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.6950286626815796}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.010202989913523197}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.6134410500526428}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.026302536949515343}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.29373109340667725}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -6.735097849741578e-05}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.0033687767572700977}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.25193163752555847}, {"token": " heroin", "logprob": -2.825220326485578e-05}, {"token": ",", "logprob